# Who you are

Just you — the parts that do not change when you change project. `gitdashy setup` asks for
the two below and nothing else.

What is being *built*, and **who owns which parts of it**, go in `project.md`, because both
are properties of a project rather than of you. On your own that file is yours; in a team it
is the team's, written once and read by everyone, and a review of a repo bound to it is told
the same thing you are. Either way it is not this file.

That distinction is worth holding: this file is loaded by every session in every repo, so a
paragraph about one product here is a paragraph in the context of work on every other. If you
find yourself writing "on the X platform we…", it belongs in that project's brief.

A few honest lines beat a long empty form.

**Name:**

**How you work:** — where you want friction and where you do not. Corrections you have
had to make more than once belong here; they are the highest-value lines in the file.

---

Anything else you add is kept. `gitdashy setup` rewrites only the sections it asked about
and leaves every other one exactly as you wrote it, so this is your file to grow — as long
as what you grow it with is true wherever you are working.
